<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rafael Zaldívar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rafael Zaldívar was a Salvadoran physician and liberal president from 1876 to 1885. His government's laws of 1881 and 1882 abolished the communal lands of indigenous villages and municipal ejidos, transferring them to private ownership and fuelling the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> estates. He left office in 1885 during a revolt against his government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-zaldivar.jpg" alt="Rafael Zaldívar" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zald%C3%ADvar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rafael Zaldívar Lazo was born in San Alejo, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>, to Apolonio Zaldívar and Antonia Lazo. He was tutored in humanities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and classical literature by José Miguel Alegría, a presbyter and deputy of the Legislative Assembly, graduated from the National College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, and in 1852 enrolled in the Faculty of Medicine of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>.</p><p>After an earthquake destroyed the university in April 1854 he moved to the University of San Carlos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, where he completed his doctorate of medicine on 23 May 1860; he later studied obstetrics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>. On 25 August 1858 he married Sara Guerra, a Nicaraguan he had met as a student in Guatemala City, and they had four children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> he served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>'s envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> until December 1865, became vice rector of the Faculty of Medicine in 1866 and Protomedic of the Nation in 1867, went as minister plenipotentiary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1869, and presided over the Legislative Assembly early in 1870. In 1871 he was Minister of War and Public Instruction under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-duenas">Francisco Dueñas</a>. When Marshal Santiago González overthrew Dueñas that year, Zaldívar fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José, Costa Rica</a>, where he opened a pharmacy and befriended President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-guardia-gutierrez">Tomás Guardia Gutiérrez</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In April 1876 the Guatemalan president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> after President Andrés del Valle and González refused to back his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, and within two weeks both resigned. Barrios set up a Junta of Notables to choose the next president, and Zaldívar, endorsed by Guardia, prevailed over General Indalecio Miranda. He became provisional president on 1 May 1876 and signed a peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> on 11 May. Attempted coups in 1877, 1879 and 1883 all failed, and his nine-year presidency was the longest in Salvadoran history until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a> held power from 1931 to 1944.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rafael Zaldívar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Laws of 1881 and 1882 under <b>Rafael Zaldívar</b> abolished the communal lands of indigenous villages and the ejidos of municipalities, opening them to private ownership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Alejo, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in 16th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.43,-87.96&amp;year=1878" aria-label="San Alejo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rafael Zaldívar"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5170 3862h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5170 3862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.43,-87.96&amp;year=1878">What was here in 1878? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
